 problem  with with what i'd say those kind of ur  texts is that they've been so  uh ripped off over the years  and mined by other you know in other  ways  the the the the sort of classic er text  of that piece which 94 is of  that it gets very hard to serve it up in  a way that doesn't  feel like you've seen it before in a  funny way  um uh so that's certainly a problem  but you know it's it's i think  that someone's gonna do it  someone is gonna do it that's for sure  completely  i'll be the first to shake hands with  the person who gets there if they do it  before i do  uh jumping into why i get to talk to you  today i want to say for everyone  watching you did a fantastic job  you and tom together are what we call um  real good  oh that's nice well thank you uh i'm  i'm always fascinated by the editing  process because it's the final rewrite  uh speaking to tom earlier he said there  were some scenes that didn't make the  finished cut  um how long for example on something  like this uh how long was your first cut  versus the finished film  i think i can't remember exactly i think  we probably took  30 minutes out something like that um  [Music]  there were a few things that that we  took out for sure  um sometimes you  find when you shoot a movie that you've  got that scene already  that actually has seen that you thought  was very different turns out  to be the same as something you've  already got  uh i mean it's a it's a mysterious and  wondrous process  editing because it's the moment when  your film starts to take  shape and i was very lucky to have billy  goldberg obviously the great billy  gomberg  this is our second film together and  he does such a beautiful job of finding  the film  that you most dreamt of when you started  you know  and i can't pay him a higher tribute  than that because  it takes but first of all it takes great  storytelling gifts  on the path of the editor and great  taste and judgment  and beautiful obviously technical  ability but  ultimately you have to have a  sensitivity to what the intent of the  filmmaker is because you're  you're roped together you know on on the  mountain  face climbing it together it's the  journey only you and your editor can  take ultimately  towards what the finished piece is  and and it's  it's a relationship that has to be  based on mutual  mutual shared sense of where the story  ultimately lies  and a willingness to be clear-eyed about  what you've actually got  what you don't actually have why you  didn't get  things that you thought were important  and maybe they aren't important  and how you can most  put to best effect things that were  important that you do have if that makes  sense  and it's a it's a beautiful  uh relationship of music making  cutting a film together you know and uh  and i'm always struck by  the way every film for a filmmaker  is a descent into the despair  of your first assembly and then a hand  over hand  ascent to the summit of  at least something you hope will be  possible  and sometimes when you've got a great  editor it's better than that  i've spoken to a lot of filmmakers um  that talk about how  after watching their first cut uh that  all they're thinking about is why did i  decide to become a filmmaker because i  am [ __ ] terrible at it  i'm finished i'll never work again  what did i ever think i was doing on  this film  oh my god this is going to be  unreleasable i mean all those things  always go through your mind there are  certain truths about filmmaking  rushes are always genius  always want to make you slit your throat  and the finished film when you get there  is always somewhere between those  two uh poles and  and uh you're right it's the final draft  of the movie  and yeah i don't think i don't i don't  think enough people actually understand  the significance  of editing and what that is to movies  but i want to switch into something i  want to switch into something else real  quick  no matter how much time and money you  have to make any movie  you're always going to be up against the  limitations of something  so can you sort of talk about the  limitations you had  on news of the world and the obstacles  you had to overcome  films are always best when there are  limitations  in my experience and that's one of the  things you know if i go to film school  and talk to young  directors that's one of the things you  have to try and get across is that that  boundaries are your friend not your  enemy because  a movie is made in a time and place  in a context within parameters whether  those parameters are  personal or or financial  or ones of time or ones of  of you know physical space whatever it  is  those parameters are where you make your  film  and if you can embrace your parameters  you  you that's where the filmmaking lies  that's where you have to surmount those  challenges  and the film becomes alive because you  do and in this case  what were the parameters what were the  boundaries what were the challenges the  challenges  were that this took place  in a grand mythic hostile environment  and we shot it in that environment and  that meant  that we were months out in the desert of  new mexico  and you know you're going to locations  like the place where the shootout takes  place where you're going  having to rope yourself up the whole  crew for two hours in the morning  and rope yourself on because it's very  dangerous and you're high up and those  huge granite rocks and you know you know  rattlesnakes and all the rest of it  so that was a challenge you've got the  challenge of a  young actress 11 years old  she's a child uh she's got  a huge part she's got to go toe to toe  with tom hanks  and she has to tell the story of her  uh her double orphaning if i can put it  that way  without the luxury of flashbacks she has  to tell it through  not and without the luxury of many words  either she has to tell it through  the power of her empathetic acting  uh so that was a boundary and you know  she transcended that  magnificently you've got always the  pressure of  time and money and the need to lead  your crew and your cast you in this  story you've got the particular  uh demands because it's a road movie in  a sense it's an  odyssey it's a journey part of that form  means that the relationship between  the news reader kid tom hanks and the  little girl  uh johanna helena zingle  takes place as they meet characters  along the way it's not a  movie where you have a cast of  characters who run through the film  you meet a character they fall away  because the journey moves on you meet  another cat they fall away  and each of those characters you have to  shape  those parts  so that they are both specific to that  character  but seen born out of the world or that  of the landscape of the film  and each character only has a limited  amount of  time for you to tell the arc of their  story for you to really believe them so  you know you  meet that uh  young boy cali in the in the buffalo  station you know in  in durand and in the course of that  relationship you have to learn  how you know how his brother was killed  by the town boss  uh how he's lost and vulnerable how he's  lit up by the power of kid's  storytelling and how he ultimately takes  his revenge  how he then moves off with kitten  johanna  and wants to be with them because he's  lonely  but most of all what he wants and needs  as kid realizes is a better future and  he can only find that  on his own and that's a touching and  beautiful  story that takes place over what four or  five scenes  nothing really but you have to feel that  these characters you  you're you're comfortable with them when  they come in the story  and you're comfortable with them leaving  the story in quite short order so that's  a  a technical challenge of the form  and then i think the other issue was  with a film like this you've got a  number of elements that you have to  balance you have to balance  the fact that it's a journey in 1870 so  they're not driving fast cars  it's going to be the journey on a wagon  through a large landscape that's got its  own  pace but you've got to  intersperse that with moments of high  excitement and adventure  and you've also got to pepper it with  jeopardy that feels real and you've got  to  convey a landscape that feels true to  those times that doesn't  [Music]  duck the difficult areas of  what 1870 was all about and ultimately  you've got to convey  uh loss  in a way that's truthful and and  powerful and emotional  and then lastly  optimism and healing that doesn't feel  sentimental but  feels earned and that's to do with  the magnificent acting at the heart of  this the magnificent relationship that  they can  chart that  that excitement that sense of danger  that  jeopardy that developing relationship  that developing  uh you know love that they begin you  know the protection that  that kid feels for this child and the  love that she obviously starts to feel  for him as a  as a father and ultimately for it to pay  off in  in in optimism because the film is about  the healing power of storytelling and  also  that these two ordinary people  they're not famous people nobody knows  who they are  they go on a journey that shows us that  it's  that each individual's decency  and the ability to recognize  where we truly belong in this new  landscape  when we get through all of this whatever  this is today  and was then in 1870  there's more that binds us together than  doesn't that's  that's really where you get to and to  make that feel  believable and earned and emotional and  true and unsentimental but moving  and optimistic that's what that's the  challenge of this movie  well i'll say you did it and uh thank  you for that great answer  and uh again nothing wish you nothing  but the best thank you so much for your  time  thank you

